ROWS = [
    dict(id="T383", d="NNA", t="How many linear regions does a typical deep ReLU network have?",
 s="A ReLU network computes a piecewise linear function; its maximal number of linear regions grows exponentially in depth (Montúfar et al.; Raghu et al.), but Hanin and Rolnick prove that for random initialization (and empirically along training) the expected number of regions meeting a line or a bounded set grows only like (number of neurons)^{input dim}, independent of depth. Prove that this polynomial count persists along gradient-descent training for a natural data model (e.g. the expected density of regions near the data stays bounded by a constant times the number of neurons), or show that training can create exponentially many regions in some natural task.",
 c="Hanin–Rolnick bound the count at initialization and for any weights drawn from distributions with bounded densities; the training-time statement is empirical.",
 src="B. Hanin and D. Rolnick, Deep ReLU Networks Have Surprisingly Few Activation Patterns, arXiv:1906.00904 (2019); M. Raghu, B. Poole, J. Kleinberg, S. Ganguli and J. Sohl-Dickstein, On the Expressive Power of Deep Neural Networks, arXiv:1606.05336 (2016)",
 loc="to verify: Hanin–Rolnick, discussion of training.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.00904",
 why="The gap between worst-case expressivity and what trained networks use is the expressivity side of the depth question.",
 st="Open as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: NeurIPS 2019 / ICML 2019 (Hanin–Rolnick)."),
    dict(id="T384", d="NNR", t="Heavy-tailed weights at infinite width: the stable process limit",
 s="If the weights of a deep fully connected network are iid with an α-stable law (α < 2) and scaled by n^{-1/α}, the infinite-width limit of the network is a stable process rather than a Gaussian process (Favaro, Fortini and Peluchetti). Characterize the training dynamics of such networks in the infinite-width limit: is there an analogue of the neural tangent kernel (a deterministic or random kernel limit), and does gradient descent in this limit perform feature learning?",
 c="The kernel in the stable limit is random (it does not concentrate). Favaro, Fortini and Peluchetti (arXiv:2206.08065) show for alpha-stable ReLU networks (shallow, to verify) that the scaling needs an extra logarithmic factor, that the infinite-width training dynamics are governed by a random alpha-stable NTK, and that gradient descent reaches zero training error at a linear rate for large width; deep networks and feature learning are open.",
 src="S. Favaro, S. Fortini and S. Peluchetti, Stable behaviour of infinitely wide deep neural networks, arXiv:2003.00394 (2020)",
 loc="to verify: Favaro et al., conclusion.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00394",
 why="Trained network weights are often heavy-tailed (Martin and Mahoney); the stable limit is the matching probabilistic model.",
 st="Open as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: AISTATS 2020."),
    dict(id='T385',
 d='NNR',
 t='The spectrum of the Fisher information of wide random networks',
 s="Empirical NTK Gram matrix of a random network of fixed depth L >= 2: inputs x_1..x_n iid N(0, I_d / d), hidden widths m, Gaussian weights in the NTK parametrization, a fixed smooth activation, scalar output, all layers' Jacobians included; n, d and m tend to infinity proportionally (n/m -> gamma, d/m -> beta). Karakida, Akaho and Amari (arXiv:1806.01316, Theorems 1, 3 and 4) compute the mean, second moment and largest eigenvalue of the Fisher information under a forward-backward independence assumption. Determine the limiting spectral distribution of the n x n Gram matrix after removing a stated finite number of spikes (from the mean of the activation and its low-order Hermite components), and its outliers, without the independence assumption; the nonzero eigenvalues of the empirical Fisher are the same up to normalization.",
 c='The two-layer and linear cases are known (Pennington and Worah; to verify). Fan and Wang (arXiv:2005.11879) prove that at width linear in the sample size, for approximately pairwise orthogonal inputs, the eigenvalue distributions of the conjugate kernel and of the NTK of a multi-layer network converge, the NTK limit being that of a linear combination of the conjugate kernels of the layers given by recursive Marcenko-Pastur fixed-point equations (the normalization they impose on the activation is to verify); Hayase and Karakida (arXiv:2006.07814) compute the spectrum of the single-sample Fisher under dynamical isometry by free probability.',
 src='R. Karakida, S. Akaho and S. Amari, Universal Statistics of Fisher Information in Deep Neural Networks: Mean Field Approach, arXiv:1806.01316 (2018)',
 loc='to verify: Karakida et al., open questions on the bulk.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.01316',
 why='The Fisher spectrum sets the conditioning of natural-gradient and second-order training, and is built from the same per-sample Jacobians whose singular values T247 asks about.',
 st='Partly solved: the deep NTK bulk at linear width is Fan and Wang; the spikes and outliers from the mean and low-order Hermite parts of the activation are open as far as the writer knows.',
 n='Fame: AISTATS 2019.',
 a=True),
    dict(id="T386", x="Retired 14 Nov 2026 (an agent): solved for Gaussian and log-concave marginals (Diakonikolas-Kontonis-Tzamos-Zarifis arXiv:2206.08918; Awasthi-Tang-Vijayaraghavan arXiv:2208.02711); replaced by T470 (a first-order method converging only to local minimax points).", d="NNT", t="Agnostic learning of a single neuron by gradient descent: the optimal approximation factor",
 s="Frei, Cao and Gu prove that gradient descent on the square loss of a single ReLU neuron, with arbitrary (agnostic) labels and suitably nice marginals, reaches error O(sqrt(OPT)) where OPT is the best neuron's error; Diakonikolas et al. give algorithms achieving O(OPT) and show computational lower bounds for OPT + ε. Determine whether gradient descent (or SGD) from a random start achieves C·OPT + ε for a constant C under Gaussian marginals, or prove that its error is necessarily ω(OPT) for some distributions.",
 c="Diakonikolas, Kontonis, Tzamos and Zarifis (arXiv:2206.08918) prove that gradient descent on the regularized square loss is a constant-factor approximate learner for monotone activations, ReLU included, under isotropic log-concave marginals, because essentially all stationary points are approximately optimal; Awasthi, Tang and Vijayaraghavan (arXiv:2208.02711) prove that gradient descent from random initialization reaches a constant factor of OPT for ReLU with moderate bias under Gaussian marginals, with finite-sample guarantees.",
 src="S. Frei, Y. Cao and Q. Gu, Agnostic Learning of a Single Neuron with Gradient Descent, arXiv:2005.14426 (2020)",
 loc="to verify: Frei–Cao–Gu, conclusion.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14426",
 why="It is the simplest test of whether gradient descent is robust to label noise in a nonconvex model.",
 st="Solved for Gaussian (and log-concave) marginals: gradient descent attains C*OPT + eps (Diakonikolas et al. 2022; Awasthi et al. 2022).",
 n="Fame: NeurIPS 2020."),
    dict(id='T387',
 d='NNL',
 t='Does a curriculum provably speed up SGD on sparse parities?',
 s='Sparse parities with a curriculum: k-sparse parity on inputs in {-1, 1}^d, k = k(d) with k fixed or growing slowly; a fraction rho of the samples have iid coordinates with mean mu (biased), the rest are uniform. Learner: a two-layer ReLU network of width poly(d), all layers trained jointly by online SGD with batch size 1, square or hinge loss, a stated initialization, step size bounded by eta and gradients computed to precision tau. Cornacchia and Mossel (arXiv:2301.13833) and Abbe, Cornacchia and Lotfi (arXiv:2306.16921, Theorems 3-5) prove positive results for layer-wise curricula and a lower bound for noisy-gradient learners depending on the number of steps, the parameter count, the gradient range and tau. Find a sufficient rho(d, k, mu) for this jointly trained architecture to reach error 1/4 in poly(d) steps, and prove a lower bound, in the same resource model (same steps, precision and gradient range), below which every such learner fails.',
 c='The upper bounds hold for particular architectures and training schedules (to verify which); the lower bounds are statistical-query and noisy-gradient bounds and may not match the achievable fractions.',
 src='E. Cornacchia and E. Mossel, A Mathematical Model for Curriculum Learning for Parities, arXiv:2301.13833 (2023); E. Abbe, E. Cornacchia and A. Lotfi, Provable Advantage of Curriculum Learning on Parity Targets with Mixed Inputs, arXiv:2306.16921 (2023)',
 loc='to verify: the open questions in the conclusions of both papers.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.16921',
 why='It is the cleanest setting where the order and mixture of training data change the complexity of SGD by more than a polynomial factor.',
 st='Open as far as the writer knows.',
 n='Fame: ICML 2023 (Cornacchia-Mossel), NeurIPS 2023 (Abbe-Cornacchia-Lotfi). Replaces a duplicate of T289 (16 Oct).',
 a=True),
    dict(id="T388", d="NNS", t="Learning from higher-order cumulants: the spiked cumulant model",
 s="Székely, Bardone, Gerace and Goldt study data whose only signal lies in a fourth-order cumulant (a non-Gaussian direction hidden in otherwise Gaussian inputs) and show a sample-complexity gap: the hypothesis test is possible with n ~ d samples information-theoretically, but low-degree methods need n ~ d² and neural networks trained by SGD learn the direction only with roughly that many. Prove that two-layer networks trained by online SGD learn the spike with n = Θ(d²) (up to logs) and fail with n = o(d²), matching the low-degree lower bound.",
 c="The low-degree lower bound is proved; in Szekely et al. the network result is numerical (quadratic sample complexity, random features at chance). Bardone and Goldt (arXiv:2404.08602) prove nearly sharp online-SGD thresholds for a single neuron and show that correlated latent variables across cumulants speed up recovery from higher-order cumulants.",
 src="E. Székely, L. Bardone, F. Gerace and S. Goldt, Learning from higher-order statistics, efficiently: hypothesis tests, random features, and neural networks, arXiv:2312.14922 (2023)",
 loc="to verify: Székely et al., conclusion.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.14922",
 why="Real data carry signal beyond second moments; this is the cleanest model of how networks extract it.",
 st="Open as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: NeurIPS 2024 (to verify)."),
    dict(id="T389", d="NNG", t="Classifying high-dimensional Gaussian mixtures with a two-layer network: the Bayes-optimal gap",
 s="For data from a mixture of two Gaussians in R^d with n = αd samples, the Bayes-optimal test error is computed by replica methods and proved for convex losses on linear classifiers (Mignacco et al.; Loureiro et al.). Prove the asymptotic test error of a two-layer network trained by gradient descent (in a regime where features are learned) on the XOR-type Gaussian mixture (four clusters, labels by the sign of the product of two coordinates), and determine whether it reaches the Bayes-optimal error at the information-theoretic threshold.",
 c="Exact asymptotics are known for linear and random-features classifiers; for trained networks only partial results exist (Ben Arous, Gheissari and Jagannath, arXiv:2206.04030, prove ballistic and diffusive limits of summary statistics of online SGD and apply them to two-layer networks on binary and XOR-type Gaussian mixtures; this gives the dynamics, not the proportional-regime test error).",
 src="B. Loureiro, G. Sicuro, C. Gerbelot, A. Pacco, F. Krzakala and L. Zdeborová, Learning Gaussian Mixtures with Generalised Linear Models: Precise Asymptotics in High-dimensions, arXiv:2106.03791 (2021); F. Mignacco, F. Krzakala, Y. M. Lu and L. Zdeborová, The role of regularization in classification of high-dimensional noisy Gaussian mixture, arXiv:2002.11544 (2020)",
 loc="to verify: Loureiro et al., conclusion on nonlinear classifiers.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.03791",
 why="Gaussian mixtures are the solvable classification model; the XOR mixture is the simplest one that needs feature learning.",
 st="Open for trained networks as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: NeurIPS 2021 (Loureiro et al.)."),
    dict(id="T390", d="NNS", t="The statistical efficiency of score matching",
 s="Koehler, Heckett and Risteski prove that the statistical efficiency of score matching for an exponential family is controlled by the log-Sobolev (or Poincaré) constant of the distribution: score matching is nearly as efficient as maximum likelihood for well-mixing distributions and can be exponentially worse for multimodal ones. Determine the sample complexity of learning a mixture of k well-separated Gaussians in R^d by (denoising) score matching at a small noise level versus by maximum likelihood, and whether annealing over noise levels (as in diffusion models) closes the gap polynomially.",
 c="Qin and Risteski (arXiv:2306.09332) prove that annealed (Gaussian-convolution) score matching has sample complexity polynomial in the dimension, the diameter of the means and the extreme eigenvalues of the covariance for a finite Gaussian mixture with shared covariance, avoiding the Poincare-constant lower bound for plain score matching. Gatmiry, Kelner and Lee (arXiv:2404.18869) learn mixtures of k identity-covariance Gaussians by a diffusion-model approach in quasi-polynomial time and samples, and Chen, Kontonis and Shah (arXiv:2404.18893) learn general mixtures with d^{poly(k/eps)} samples by score matching.",
 src="F. Koehler, A. Heckett and A. Risteski, Statistical Efficiency of Score Matching: The View from Isoperimetry, arXiv:2210.00726 (2022)",
 loc="to verify: Koehler et al., conclusion.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.00726",
 why="Score matching is the training objective of diffusion models; its efficiency is governed by functional inequalities.",
 st="Partly solved: annealing closes the gap polynomially for shared covariance (Qin-Risteski); the exact rates against maximum likelihood are open.",
 n="Fame: ICLR 2023 (oral, to verify)."),
    dict(id="T391", d="NNS", t="Diffusion models adapt to the intrinsic dimension of the data manifold",
 s="When the data lie on (or near) a k-dimensional smooth manifold in R^d, score-based diffusion models empirically generate well with sample complexity depending on k, not d. Prove that a diffusion model with a score estimated by empirical risk minimization over a natural network class, run with a standard discretization, achieves distribution error (e.g. in Wasserstein distance) n^{-c/k} up to logs, with the number of steps polylogarithmic or polynomial in k rather than d, and with matching minimax lower bounds.",
 c="Results with intrinsic-dimension rates exist under assumptions (to verify 2024-25 work, e.g. Tang and Yang; Azangulov, Deligiannidis and Rousseau). For the discretization, Potaptchik, Azangulov and Deligiannidis (arXiv:2410.09046) prove that the number of steps needed for convergence in KL is linear, up to logarithms, in the intrinsic dimension k, and that this is sharp; Li and Yan (arXiv:2405.14861) show that DDPM with a particular choice of coefficients converges at rate O(k^2/sqrt(T)) and that with general coefficients a dependence on the ambient dimension is unavoidable. Combining these with a score learned by ERM over a network class into one end-to-end rate is the open part.",
 src="I. Azangulov, G. Deligiannidis and J. Rousseau, Convergence of Diffusion Models Under the Manifold Hypothesis in High-Dimensions, arXiv:2409.18804 (2024)",
 loc="to verify: Azangulov et al., open questions.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.18804",
 why="It is the statistical explanation of why diffusion models work on images, whose dimension is far above their intrinsic dimension.",
 st="Partly solved: the discretization depends only on the intrinsic dimension (linear and sharp); the end-to-end rate with an ERM-trained network score is open as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: the manifold question is the standard theory question for diffusion models."),
    dict(id='T392',
 d='NNS',
 t='What classifier-free guidance does to the sampled distribution',
 s='Classifier-free guidance on a Gaussian mixture with common nondegenerate covariance Sigma, K components with positive weights and centers mu_1..mu_K, exact conditional and unconditional scores of the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck forward process, and the guided score s_uncond + w (s_cond - s_uncond), w > 1. Wu et al. (arXiv:2403.01639, Theorems 3.3 and 4.2) prove that guidance raises the posterior class probability and lowers the differential entropy of the DDIM (probability-flow) output; Bradley and Nakkiran (arXiv:2408.09000) solve a Gaussian example in which the DDIM endpoint, the DDPM endpoint and the tilted law p(x)^(1-w) p(x | c)^w all differ. In continuous time and for the DDPM (stochastic) sampler, give the law of the output, or bounds on its entropy and on its distance from the tilted law, as explicit functions of w and of the center separation, and compare them with the DDIM endpoint.',
 c='Results exist for Gaussian mixtures and for linear diffusions. Chidambaram et al. (arXiv:2409.13074) prove for mixtures of compactly supported distributions and for Gaussian mixtures that guidance does not sample the tilted distribution, that larger guidance pushes samples toward the boundary of the conditional support, and that with any score error a large enough guidance samples off the support; Bradley and Nakkiran (arXiv:2408.09000) prove that in the SDE limit guidance equals a DDIM predictor for the conditional law combined with a Langevin corrector for a gamma-powered law.',
 src='Y. Wu, M. Chen, Z. Li, M. Wang and Y. Wei, Theoretical Insights for Diffusion Guidance: A Case Study for Gaussian Mixture Models, arXiv:2403.01639 (2024)',
 loc='to verify: Wu et al., conclusion.',
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.01639',
 why='Guidance is used in essentially every text-to-image system; what distribution it samples from is not known.',
 st='Open beyond special models.',
 n='Fame: ICML 2024 (to verify).',
 a=True),
    dict(id="T393", d="NNS", t="Minimax rates of generative adversarial networks with trained networks",
 s="Liang proves rates for GAN-type density estimation in adversarial (integral probability metric) losses, showing GANs can achieve minimax rates when generator and discriminator classes are rich enough and the min-max problem is solved exactly. Prove a rate for a GAN with neural generator and discriminator whose parameters are computed by an actual gradient-based algorithm (e.g. simultaneous gradient descent-ascent from random initialization) for a natural target class, matching the minimax rate for that class up to logs.",
 c="All statistical rates assume an exact or approximate global solution of the min-max problem (compare T312).",
 src="T. Liang, How Well Generative Adversarial Networks Learn Distributions, arXiv:1811.03179 (2018)",
 loc="to verify: Liang, conclusion (optimization as open).",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.03179",
 why="It joins the statistics and the optimization of adversarial generative modelling into one guarantee.",
 st="Open as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: JMLR 2021."),
    dict(id="T394", d="NNS", t="Posterior collapse in variational autoencoders",
 s="A VAE with a flexible decoder can ignore its latent code ('posterior collapse'): the approximate posterior equals the prior and the decoder models the data alone. Lucas, Tucker, Grosse and Norouzi show that in linear VAEs collapse is caused by local maxima of the marginal likelihood related to probabilistic PCA and the observation-noise parameter. Characterize, for a nonlinear VAE with a two-layer decoder, the set of data distributions and noise levels at which the ELBO's global optimum (or every local optimum reachable by gradient ascent) collapses some latent dimensions, and prove the threshold.",
 c="The linear case is solved; nonlinear analyses are limited (to verify).",
 src="J. Lucas, G. Tucker, R. Grosse and M. Norouzi, Don't Blame the ELBO! A Linear VAE Perspective on Posterior Collapse, arXiv:1911.02469 (2019)",
 loc="to verify: Lucas et al., discussion.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02469",
 why="Posterior collapse is the main failure mode of latent-variable deep generative models.",
 st="Open for nonlinear models as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: NeurIPS 2019."),
    dict(id="T395", d="NNS", t="Deep-learning PDE solvers: does training beat the curse of dimensionality?",
 s="E, Han and Jentzen's deep BSDE method solves semilinear parabolic PDEs in hundreds of dimensions; Grohs, Hornung, Jentzen and von Wurstemberger prove that deep networks can approximate solutions of Kolmogorov PDEs (e.g. Black-Scholes) with size polynomial in d and 1/ε. Prove that the full method — sampling, a network class and a training algorithm actually used (SGD) — computes the solution at a point to accuracy ε with cost polynomial in d and 1/ε for a natural class of Kolmogorov or semilinear PDEs.",
 c="Approximation and generalization parts are proved; the optimization part (finding the network by SGD) is the gap.",
 src="W. E, J. Han and A. Jentzen, Deep learning-based numerical methods for high-dimensional parabolic partial differential equations and backward stochastic differential equations, arXiv:1706.04702 (2017); P. Grohs, F. Hornung, A. Jentzen and P. von Wurstemberger, A proof that artificial neural networks overcome the curse of dimensionality in the numerical approximation of Black-Scholes partial differential equations, arXiv:1809.02362 (2018)",
 loc="to verify: Grohs et al., introduction (training not covered).",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.02362",
 why="It is the main applied-probability use of deep learning (high-dimensional option pricing, stochastic control); its full guarantee is missing.",
 st="Open (training part).",
 n="Fame: E–Han–Jentzen (PNAS 2018 version) is very widely cited; Grohs et al. in Memoirs AMS."),
    dict(id="T396", d="NNA", t="Oversmoothing in deep graph neural networks: a sharp theory",
 s="As a graph neural network gets deeper, node representations converge to a common value (oversmoothing): Oono and Suzuki prove exponential convergence to a low-dimensional subspace for GCNs with ReLU and weights of small enough singular value. Determine, for random or trained weights at standard initialization and for message passing with attention (graph attention networks), whether oversmoothing occurs at an exponential rate, and characterize the depth at which node classification accuracy on a stochastic block model degrades.",
 c="Wu et al. prove that attention does not prevent oversmoothing (to verify 2305.16102); finite-depth analyses on contextual SBMs exist.",
 src="K. Oono and T. Suzuki, Graph Neural Networks Exponentially Lose Expressive Power for Node Classification, arXiv:1905.10947 (2019); X. Wu, A. Ajorlou, Z. Wu and A. Jadbabaie, Demystifying Oversmoothing in Attention-Based Graph Neural Networks, arXiv:2305.16102 (2023)",
 loc="to verify: Oono–Suzuki, discussion of the singular-value condition.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.10947",
 why="Oversmoothing limits the depth of graph networks; it is a question about products of random Markov-type operators.",
 st="Open (sharp form).",
 n="Fame: ICLR 2020; NeurIPS 2023."),
    dict(id="T397", d="NNA", t="Graph neural networks for community detection at the Kesten–Stigum threshold",
 s="Chen, Li and Bruna propose line-graph neural networks for community detection in the stochastic block model and observe performance near the information-theoretic thresholds. Prove that a graph neural network of depth O(log n) with trained (or explicitly chosen) weights achieves nontrivial correlation with the communities in the two-community sparse SBM for every signal-to-noise ratio above the Kesten–Stigum threshold, and determine whether a GNN trained by gradient descent from random initialization does so.",
 c="Message passing algorithms (non-backtracking spectral methods, belief propagation) reach the threshold; GNNs can simulate them with chosen weights in principle; the trained-GNN statement is open (to verify). Baranwal (arXiv:2607.16676) proves a Kesten-Stigum dichotomy for a fixed message-passing classifier on the sparse contextual SBM: below the threshold extra depth beyond O(log(1/eps)) changes the error by less than eps, above it depth drives the error down geometrically to a branching-process floor; the weights there are derived, not trained, and node features are present.",
 src="Z. Chen, L. Li and J. Bruna, Supervised Community Detection with Line Graph Neural Networks, arXiv:1705.08415 (2017)",
 loc="to verify: Chen–Li–Bruna, discussion.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.08415",
 why="It tests whether learned message passing reaches the sharp statistical thresholds that belief propagation reaches.",
 st="Open (trained case).",
 n="Fame: ICLR 2019."),
    dict(id="T398", d="NNA", t="Can graph neural networks beat greedy algorithms on random combinatorial problems?",
 s="Angelini and Ricci-Tersenghi show that a GNN-based solver for maximum independent set on random d-regular graphs performs worse than a simple greedy algorithm, and Boettcher similarly for max-cut. By the overlap gap property, local algorithms cannot find independent sets of density above (1+1/√2)·(log d)/d on random d-regular graphs for large d (Gamarnik and Sudan; improved to (1+ε)·(log d)/d by Rahman and Virág), and Gamarnik notes that this covers constant-depth GNNs, which were reported to solve such problems (Schuetz, Brubaker and Katzgraber). Determine whether GNNs of depth growing with n, trained by gradient methods, can exceed the greedy density (log d)/d on random d-regular graphs, or prove a lower bound for this class.",
 c="The local-algorithm barrier is proved; Gamarnik (arXiv:2306.02555) shows that the overlap gap property bars a broad range of GNN architectures on these random graphs because they are local. Message passing whose depth grows with n is not covered.",
 src="M. C. Angelini and F. Ricci-Tersenghi, Modern graph neural networks do worse than classical greedy algorithms in solving combinatorial optimization problems like maximum independent set, arXiv:2206.13211 (2022); D. Gamarnik, Barriers for the performance of graph neural networks (GNN) in discrete random structures, arXiv:2306.02555 (2023)",
 loc="to verify: Gamarnik, the GNN barrier paper's open questions.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.13211",
 why="It is the clean test of learned algorithms against the overlap gap barrier from spin-glass theory.",
 st="Open for deep GNNs.",
 n="Fame: Nature Machine Intelligence 2023 (Angelini–Ricci-Tersenghi)."),
    dict(id="T399", d="NNA", t="When does next-token prediction fail to learn planning tasks?",
 s="Bachmann and Nagarajan show that teacher-forced next-token prediction fails on a simple path-finding task on star graphs ('the Clever Hans cheat'), while other objectives succeed. Prove a separation: for a natural class of planning or lookahead tasks, any autoregressive model trained by teacher forcing on next-token loss (with a natural architecture and gradient training) has error bounded below by a constant, while a model trained with a multi-token or reverse-order objective learns the task with polynomial samples.",
 c="The star-graph task is analyzed empirically and partly theoretically; a general separation theorem is open. Frydenlund (arXiv:2410.13779) shows the path-star task is learnable with teacher forcing in other settings and gives RASP constructions, locating part of the failure in the representation; Huang et al. (arXiv:2604.11912) prove for a simplified two-layer transformer on a star graph that multi-token prediction induces backward path reconstruction through a gradient decoupling that next-token prediction lacks.",
 src="G. Bachmann and V. Nagarajan, The Pitfalls of Next-Token Prediction, arXiv:2403.06963 (2024)",
 loc="to verify: Bachmann–Nagarajan, conclusion.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06963",
 why="It asks whether the training objective of language models limits what they can learn, independent of scale.",
 st="Open as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: ICML 2024."),
    dict(id="T400", d="NNL", t="Regret of neural contextual bandits beyond the lazy regime",
 s="Zhou, Li and Gu prove that NeuralUCB, a UCB algorithm on the neural tangent features of an overparameterized network, achieves regret Õ(d̃ √T), where d̃ is the effective dimension of the NTK Gram matrix. Prove a regret bound for a neural bandit algorithm that learns features (e.g. a two-layer network in a regime where features move) that improves on all kernel-based bounds for a natural reward class, such as a single-index reward with unknown direction.",
 c="Kernel-regime analyses give bounds governed by the NTK effective dimension, which can be polynomial in T; feature-learning bounds are lacking (to verify).",
 src="D. Zhou, L. Li and Q. Gu, Neural Contextual Bandits with UCB-based Exploration, arXiv:1911.04462 (2019)",
 loc="to verify: Zhou et al., conclusion.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.04462",
 why="Exploration with learned representations is the decision-making counterpart of the feature-learning question.",
 st="Open as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: ICML 2020."),
    dict(id="T401", d="NNG", t="Do deep ensembles approximate Bayesian model averaging?",
 s="Wilson and Izmailov argue that deep ensembles (independently trained networks averaged) are a practical approximation to the Bayesian posterior predictive. Prove, in a solvable model (e.g. wide two-layer networks or random-features regression with random initializations), a quantitative relation between the predictive distribution of an ensemble of M gradient-trained networks and the Bayesian posterior predictive under a natural prior, including when ensembles are better or worse calibrated than the posterior.",
 c="In the NTK limit, ensembles of trained networks correspond to a Gaussian process whose posterior differs from the NNGP posterior (He, Lakshminarayanan and Teh, to verify); outside special limits there is no comparison theorem.",
 src="A. G. Wilson and P. Izmailov, Bayesian Deep Learning and a Probabilistic Perspective of Generalization, arXiv:2002.08791 (2020)",
 loc="to verify: Wilson–Izmailov, discussion of ensembles.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.08791",
 why="Ensembles are the most reliable uncertainty method in practice; whether and why they are Bayesian is open.",
 st="Open as far as the writer knows.",
 n="Fame: NeurIPS 2020."),
    dict(id="T402", d="NNS", t="When does the kernel Stein discrepancy control convergence?",
 s="Gorham and Mackey prove that the kernel Stein discrepancy (KSD) with the inverse multiquadric kernel controls weak convergence to a target that is distantly dissipative, while Gaussian and Matérn kernels fail to detect non-convergence in dimension d ≥ 3. Characterize the kernels and targets (e.g. heavy-tailed or multimodal) for which KSD convergence to zero implies weak convergence, and give quantitative rates relating KSD to Wasserstein distance with explicit dependence on dimension.",
 c="Sufficient conditions are known. Barp, Simon-Gabriel, Girolami and Mackey (arXiv:2209.12835) give new necessary and sufficient conditions for separation and weak-convergence control and the first KSDs that exactly metrize weak convergence; Kanagawa, Barp, Gretton and Mackey (arXiv:2211.05408) show standard KSDs fail to control moments and build diffusion KSDs that characterize q-Wasserstein convergence for each q > 0. Tight dimension-dependent comparisons remain open.",
 src="J. Gorham and L. Mackey, Measuring Sample Quality with Kernels, arXiv:1703.01717 (2017)",
 loc="to verify: Gorham–Mackey, open questions.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.01717",
 why="KSD is the standard tool for measuring the quality of samples from learned or approximate samplers and underlies SVGD (T310).",
 st="Open (quantitative form).",
 n="Fame: ICML 2017."),
]
